60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option B is the most logical for the morning rush. If we use double-tier racks, we increase capacity and remove the ""shoving match"" for specific spots.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,Option A is the only one that addresses the financial gap. It creates a revenue stream for the building without requiring a separate levy.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,George,,Option C is the fairest. It completely erases the historical bias I found in the minutes and ensures no one is permanently privileged.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,Option A creates a class system based on who can pay or who got there first. Option B is a free-for-all that could lead to daily friction.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Mio,,Option A sounds like a paperwork nightmare! Collecting monthly fees for individual spots? No way! That's way too much work for one person!,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Shunichi, Option B isn't a free-for-all if the racks are designed for efficiency. It solves the physical congestion and the rain exposure issue.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Mio, the administrative burden is a valid point, but the lack of funds is a structural risk. However, if B increases capacity, it might reduce the need for payment.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,George,,"@Asuka, while B is efficient, it doesn't provide the security some residents want. Option C provides a guaranteed spot for a year, which is a fair compromise.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@George, a lottery is a gamble. If someone gets the worst spot for a year, they'll spend twelve months staring daggers at the person who got the best one.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Mio,,Option C is way better than A! You only have to do the paperwork once a year instead of every single month. That's much more sustainable!,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"Option C doesn't solve the physical congestion. We'll still have the same bottleneck in the morning, just with different people in the spots.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"I'm reconsidering. If Option B increases the number of spaces, it removes the scarcity that makes the allocation so contentious. That's a logical solution.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,George,,I still believe a lottery is the only way to prove to the residents that the association is no longer playing favorites. It's a symbolic reset.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"If we want to avoid a toxic atmosphere, we should remove the ""ownership"" of spots entirely. Option B removes the source of the conflict: the spot itself.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Mio,,"I love Option B! No one ""owns"" a spot, so there's no list to maintain, no money to collect, and no lottery to organize. It's the easiest!","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Asuka,,It seems we have a consensus that physical efficiency and low administration are priorities. Option B is the most rational choice.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,"I agree. While I wanted a financial solution, the social risk of a ""paid"" system and the administrative burden make Option B the most stable choice.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,George,,"I am hesitant, but if Option B removes the concept of ""assigned spots"" entirely, it does achieve my goal of ending the favoritism.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"Exactly. When no one owns the space, there is nothing to be jealous of. It's the most sophisticated way to maintain peace in the lobby.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Mio,,"Yay! Let's go with B! It's the most energetic and simple way to do it, and it won't burn out whoever has to manage it!","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
